{% comment %}
  Shared framing for every review/<tier>.md.liquid prompt in this
  bundle. The triad invariant (dsl-syntax.md §9) means a review prompt
  receives identical context to the paired generation prompt plus
  `draft` — this partial is what stays constant across every tier's
  review pass; the tier-specific checklist (ported from
  seed-docs/siege-prompts/review_*.md where one exists) is the only
  part that varies.
{% endcomment %}
You are reviewing the draft below against the context you have been
given — the same context the generation pass that produced it saw,
plus this draft itself. You are not regenerating; you are judging.

{% comment %}
  `draft` is passed explicitly by every call site rather than read
  from the caller's scope (ORC-201). `{% render %}` without arguments
  isolates a partial's scope from its caller's — `deps/solid`'s
  `RenderTag` builds an empty vars map — so for as long as the call
  sites were bare, this partial said "the draft below" above a draft
  it had no way to print. `draft` is a plain string
  (`Catapult.Generation.ContextAssembly.draft_variable/2` returns the
  body or ""), so it interpolates directly; a collection would need
  the `{% for %}` treatment `partials/_architecture_framing` gives
  `feedback` (ORC-193).
{% endcomment %}
Draft under review:

{{ draft }}

Output a single `<review>` element per `schemas/review.xsd`: a
`verdict` attribute (`approve` or `revise`), a `<summary>`, a
`<score>` — an integer 0-100 — and zero or more `<finding>` elements,
each with an `id` (short, unique within this review — `f1`, `f2`, …),
a `severity` (`blocking` or `advisory`), a `<location>` (the element
or attribute path inside the draft the finding is about), and a
`<comment>`.

* `<score>` buckets: 0-30 fundamental rework, 31-60 structural fixes,
  61-85 minor refinements, 86-100 ready. Score what you actually read,
  not what the verdict implies — a `revise` is not automatically
  low-scoring, and an `approve` is not automatically high-scoring.
* `revise` requires at least one `blocking` finding — a revise verdict
  with nothing blocking about it gives the next pass nothing to act on.
* `approve` may still carry `advisory` findings; they are not
  read as revision demands, use them for improvements you noticed but
  that don't block this draft moving forward.
* Every `id` must be unique within this review — it is what an
  individual finding gets anchored to when it is projected as a PR
  comment, and a reused id is ambiguous about which comment it names.
* Ground every finding in the context you were given, not in a prior
  about what drafts like this "usually" contain. If a context section
  this checklist references is missing from your prompt, do not flag
  drift against it — you have no baseline to compare against.
* Do not include commentary outside the `<review>` element.
